## The Gallery Gap Decision

This is the structural choice inside the hidden halo category. It determines how deep and visible the hidden halo effect is.

Every hidden halo ring has a "gallery gap" — the space between the bottom of the center stone and the hidden halo diamonds. A smaller gap means the halo diamonds sit closer to the girdle. A larger gap means the diamonds sit lower in a wider groove with more decorative detailing in between.

**Petite hidden halo** settings have a tight, narrow gallery gap. The diamonds sit very close to the girdle. From the side, you see a thin ring of sparkle right under the stone. It's the cleanest, most modern version of the hidden halo.

**Standard solitaire-plus hidden halo** settings have slightly more space in the gallery and typically carry more diamond weight. The halo ring is still hidden from above but it's more prominent from the side. This is the step between petite and lace bridge.

**Lace bridge hidden halo** settings add decorative detail — milgrain, filigree, or pavé — in the gallery space between the hidden halo and the shank. From the side, the whole gallery area is detailed. These rings look like solitaires from above but reveal an intricate structure from the side. This is the most vintage-inspired hidden halo option.

The right choice depends on how much you want the hidden halo to show when the ring is viewed at an angle. Petite: subtle. Standard: moderate. Lace bridge: dramatic from the side.

